/*! \page psfts_algo_montecarlo_page  Monte Carlo

\ref psfts_algo_brownian_page    (Prev) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref psfts_algo_compressor_page  (Next) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

The PSCF implementation of MC sampling allows the user to specify a set
of Monte Carlo move types to be used during an MC simulation. When more
than one type of move is specified, one of the move types is chosen at
random at the beginning of each step, using probabilities that are
specified by the user.  

\section psfts_algo_montecarlo_overview_sec  Overview

The general algorithm for each attempted step of a PSCF MC simulation 
is thus as follows:

   - Choose a type of MC move at random from among a set of choices
     specified in the parameter file.

   - Use the chosen MC move to generate a random change in the fields.

   - Apply the chosen compressor algorithm to adjust the real pressure-like
     field \f$ W_{+}({\bf r}) \f$ so as to again satisfy the partial
     saddle-point (i.e., SCF incompressibility) condition.

   - Evaluate the Hamiltonian in the new partial saddle point state.

   - Use a Metropolis criterion that is designed to satisfy detailed
     balance to decide whether to accept or reject the move.

   - If the move is rejected, restore the field configuration that
     existed before the beginning of the attempted move.

The block of the parameter file for an MC simulation that contains
information about MC moves, which is labelled "McMoveManager", contains
one or more subblocks that are associated with different types of move.
The parameter file block associated with each such MC move type contains
a parameter named "probability" that specifies the probability of that
type of move being chosen at the start of each step.

\section psfts_algo_montecarlo_available_sec  Algorithms

PSCF currently provides the following MC step algorithms:
<ul>
  <li> \subpage rp_RealMove_page "RealMcMove" : A simple MC move that
  generates statistically independent random changes of
  \f$ W_{-} \f$ all grid points </li>
  <li> \subpage rp_ForceBiasMove_page "ForceBiasMove": A "smart MC" 
  or "force bias" MC move that uses an explicit Euler BD step to
  generate a proposed MC move. </li>
  <li> \subpage rp_ShiftMove_page "ShiftMove": This move generates
  a proposed MC move by applying a rigid translation of the entire
  current field configuration.
  <li> \subpage rp_BdMove_page "BdMove": A BD move simply runs a 
  short BD simulation of a specified number of steps, and always 
  accepts the result. This move does not satisfy detailed balance.
</ul>
We recommend the ForceBiasMove as the workhorse method for MC 
sampling, among moves that satisfy the detailed balance condition.
The BdMove (which does not obey detailed balance) is provided to 
allow users to mix BD and MC sampling techniques, by combining a 
BdMove with one or more other more specialized MC moves.

\section psfts_algo_montecarlo_mcbd_sec  MC vs. BD

Acceptance rates for "global" MC moves that satisfy detailed 
balance generally decrease rapidly with increasing system size.  
The decrease of acceptance rates with increasing system size is 
particularly severe with the simple real-space move (RealMcMove), 
but also occurs to a lesser degree for the force-bias (ForceBiasMoveMove). 
Because BD simulation does not suffer from any analogous problem, 
this phenomena causes MC to generally become rapidly less efficient 
than BD for sufficiently large systems. We thus generally recommend 
the use of BD or hybrid simulations rather than true MC simulation 
for 3D systems with roughly \f$ 10^{4} \f$ or more grid points. 
The BdMove algorithm is provided to allow implementation of 
hybrid BD/MC simulations for large systems in special situations 
in which occasional application of a specialized MC move can speed 
up BD sampling.

Monte Carlo move used in molecular systems are usually spatially
local moves, often involving only a single molecule, for which the 
resulting change in energy can be rapidly evaluated by analyzing
only a small part of the simulated system.  Unfortunately, the 
dependence of the field-theoretic Hamiltonian functional on the 
fields is inherently non-local. As a result, in order to evaluate 
the change in the Hamiltonian induced by any change in the fields
one generally must solve the modified diffusion equation for the
entire system. This non-locality is what makes it difficult to
implement efficient MC algorithms for field theoretic simulations
of large systems.

One advantage of MC over BD simulation is that, because proper 
MC algorithms satisfy a detailed balance condition, MC simulations
generally do no exhibit any systematic sampling error. An infinitely 
long MC simulation would, in in the absence of round-off error, yield 
exact results for any average value obtained from a particular model 
on a specified computational mesh.  The most important class of errors 
in MC simulations are thus usually true statistical errors arising 
from the finite length of a simulation, which can be quantified by 
standard methods of analyzing time sequences.

BD simulations instead exhibit a systematic error arising from
discretization of a time-like coordinate in a diffusion model. The
magnitude of this error increases with increases in the mobility 
parameter, but is not particularly sensitive to changes in system
size. Results of MC simulations of small systems can sometimes be 
useful for comparison when testing new BD algorithms, and when 
evaluating systematic errors in BD algorithms. Systematic errors 
in BD simulations of large systems can also be estimated by running 
and comparing simulations with several different values of the 
mobility parameter.

